WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.400
I can’t believe I am saying this, but today we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.460
going to read human minds by using AI.&nbsp;
Yes, that’s right. That really happened.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.520
So how do we read brains? Well here,&nbsp;
we are mainly interested in peaceful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:26.940
non-invasive method, and that is going to be&nbsp;
an fMRI. Functional magnetic resonance imaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.000
this is where you go into a tube, are asked&nbsp;
to stay still, and your brain activity will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.420
be read. We get a result that is something&nbsp;
like this. So what is this good for? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.740
these are cross-sectional views&nbsp;
of the brain, slices if you will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.620
and not only that, but blood flow within&nbsp;
the brain can also be highlighted.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.640
This gives us an idea about what parts of the&nbsp;
brain are activated and when. So for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.160
we can show someone an image of a&nbsp;
familiar face and an unfamiliar one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.500
and see if there is a difference in the&nbsp;
brain activity, and what that difference is.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.440
That sounds great. However, not so fast.&nbsp;
With these peaceful non-invasive techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.740
the readings are not as precise, and there is so&nbsp;
much more noise. And now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.860
because this paper did something that I did&nbsp;
not think humanity would ever be able to do.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:32.160
So here is the mind reading experiment they&nbsp;
propose. Chuck some folks into an fMRI machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:39.660
show them images, and then make a brain reading.&nbsp;
Now this brain reading would have to be converted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:45.840
to an image, not just this noisy blood flow&nbsp;
information, which sounds almost impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:52.800
And it is almost impossible. A previous work&nbsp;
from just 5 years ago did something like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.520
These images went in, and this came out. This&nbsp;
is a reconstruction of what scientists think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.160
the brain saw. In terms of shapes, there is&nbsp;
perhaps some correlation between the two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:11.940
perhaps they are related, but I feel like I am&nbsp;
grasping for straws here. And then, four years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:18.780
later, two really promising works appeared that&nbsp;
could reconstruct these images from the brain. An&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:25.680
incredible scientific achievement, however,&nbsp;
still very blurry images. In this new work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.660
at the risk of simplifying a little, the&nbsp;
idea is to take these brain readings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:38.280
and plug them into the legendary text to image&nbsp;
AI system, Stable Diffusion. So what do we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:44.580
as a result? And now is the point where I fell&nbsp;
off the chair when reading this paper. Here are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:51.960
the new results. Wow! Now we’re talking! These&nbsp;
are so much better. The readings show a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:57.600
correlation of the real images that have been&nbsp;
shown to the subjects. And wait it gets better!

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.660
It also works for video! That means that we&nbsp;
can show these subjects all kinds of videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.880
and after the brain reading reconstruction,&nbsp;
we don’t get back exactly what was shown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.540
but the results are so much&nbsp;
higher quality than before.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:17.940
Now, evaluating these results is a tough&nbsp;
matter, there are fundamental questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:25.140
for a perfect reconstruction, are these two&nbsp;
supposed to be the same? Good question. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:30.660
the fMRI machine and the reconstruction&nbsp;
algorithm both have their limits. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.880
list goes on. A healthy dose of skepticism&nbsp;
is a hallmark of the Wise Scholar.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:42.480
However, for now, we can compare the reconstructed&nbsp;
results to the ground truth through, one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.740
visual inspection. Or, two, through mathematical&nbsp;
ways of comparing images to each other. With both,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.600
this new technique has been shown to be&nbsp;
so much better than the previous papers.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:00.000
And as every good paper does, this one&nbsp;
also raises important new questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.260
too. For instance, does it work for a diverse&nbsp;
set of topics? It does. And what I really want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:13.440
to know - how similar would these images be&nbsp;
for different people? What do you think? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:19.560
I am delighted to tell you that there was an&nbsp;
experiment for that too. Absolutely lovely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.540
It almost feels like we are living in&nbsp;
a science fiction world. My brain is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.760
also bubbling with ideas. I wonder what&nbsp;
that would look like in a video form?

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:36.180
So, is this work perfect? The&nbsp;
answer is no. Not perfect. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:40.740
even close. There are still lots of&nbsp;
limitations and many failure cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:46.380
and we should all exercise a little critical&nbsp;
thinking for works like this. But once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:53.820
the help of AI research makes the almost&nbsp;
impossible seem possible. What a time to be alive!

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.087
Now, note that this video came way later than&nbsp;
it could have. That is not great for views,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.087 --> 00:05:32.100
but that’s not what we are after. We wait until&nbsp;
the paper is sufficiently reviewed, the evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:42.180
is thoroughly checked, and I only want to show it&nbsp;
to you then. That is the only thing that matters.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.260
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

